description:
Three BFS implementations use the loop+break-on-empty anti-pattern instead of the standard while-loop:

1. src/backend.rs:155-191 (FileNodeBackend::query_subtree default impl):
   loop {
       if frontier.is_empty() || depth_remaining == 0 {
           break;
       }
       depth_remaining = depth_remaining.saturating_sub(1);
       ...
   }

2. src/memory.rs:593-612 (MemoryBackend::get_ancestors):
   loop {
       if frontier.is_empty() {
           break;
       }
       let mut next_frontier = Vec::new();
       ...
   }

3. src/memory.rs:635-652 (MemoryBackend::get_descendant_ids):
   loop {
       if frontier.is_empty() {
           break;
       }
       ...
   }

What idiomatic Rust would do: 'while !frontier.is_empty() && depth_remaining > 0 { ... }' for case 1, 'while !frontier.is_empty() { ... }' for cases 2 and 3. The condition belongs in the loop header, not in a guard-break at the top of the body. clippy::while_let_loop and the broader 'use while when you have a condition' idiom both push toward this.

Why it matters: a 'loop { if cond { break; } }' shape obscures the loop invariant. A reader has to scan into the body to find out when the loop terminates. The 'while' form documents 'this loop runs as long as the frontier is non-empty' at the point of definition. Maintainability cost is low but real — easy to misread (e.g. a future contributor adds a 'continue' that bypasses the break) and easy to miss when refactoring the iteration semantics.

Canonical comparison: jmap-mail-server has zero 'loop { if X.is_empty() { break; } }' instances in src/. This pattern is filenode-specific.

Propagation note: not applicable — canonical does not have this pattern, no upstream change required. The fix is local to this crate, three sites.
